    studySequence: [
      { step: '1. Anatomical classification', detail: 'SUN nomenclature and the targeted workup that follows from each compartment.' },
      { step: '2. Anterior uveitis', detail: 'Acute vs chronic, HLA-B27 spectrum, Fuchs, Posner-Schlossman.' },
      { step: '3. Intermediate uveitis', detail: 'Pars planitis, sarcoid, multiple sclerosis associations.' },
      { step: '4. Posterior uveitis', detail: 'Toxoplasma, CMV, ARN, PORN, syphilis, TB, sarcoid.' },
      { step: '5. Panuveitis', detail: 'VKH, sympathetic ophthalmia, Behçet, sarcoid.' },
      { step: '6. Treatment ladder', detail: 'Steroids, methotrexate, mycophenolate, anti-TNF biologics.' },
    ],
    sampleQuestion: 'A patient with ankylosing spondylitis presents with acute unilateral red eye. What type of uveitis?',
    sampleAnswer: 'Acute anterior uveitis (AAU), typically HLA-B27 associated. Treat with topical steroids and cycloplegics; refer for rheumatology workup.',
    faqs: [
      { q: 'What is the first investigation in newly diagnosed uveitis?', a: 'A targeted workup based on the anatomical compartment and clinical pattern. Routine baseline includes CBC, ESR, CRP, ACE, syphilis serology, mantoux/IGRA and CXR for sarcoid.' },
      { q: 'When to start systemic immunosuppression in uveitis?', a: 'When steroid taper below 7.5 mg/day prednisolone cannot be achieved without flare, or when sight-threatening posterior involvement is present.' },
      { q: 'Adalimumab indications in uveitis?', a: 'FDA-approved for non-infectious intermediate, posterior and panuveitis. First-line biologic in Behçet ocular disease.' },
      { q: 'How to differentiate viral retinitis subtypes?', a: 'CMV: indolent, AIDS, pizza-pie. ARN: immunocompetent, fulminant, VZV/HSV. PORN: immunocompromised, outer retinal, VZV.' },
      { q: 'Best book for uveitis?', a: 'Foster & Vitale Diagnosis and Treatment of Uveitis; Nussenblatt Uveitis; Kanski for exam-level coverage.' },
    ],
  },
  oculoplastics: {
    name: 'Oculoplastics',
    questionCount: '250+',
    glossaryCategory: 'Oculoplastics',
    mcqTopic: 'Oculoplastics & Orbit',
    productIds: ['eyelids-notes', 'high-yield-mcqs'],
    relatedExams: [{ slug: 'frcophth', name: 'FRCOphth' }, { slug: 'dnb', name: 'DNB' }, { slug: 'faico', name: 'FAICO' }, { slug: 'neet-ss', name: 'NEET-SS' }],
    seoTitle: 'Oculoplastics MCQs & Study Guide — Lids, Lacrimal, Orbit',
    seoDescription: 'Oculoplastics study guide for ophthalmology exams. 250+ MCQs on lid malpositions, lacrimal disease, orbital inflammation, lid tumours and thyroid eye disease.',
    overview: 'Oculoplastics, lacrimal and orbital disease form the third sub-specialty by exam weight in most modern PG exams. The topic combines anatomy, surgical principles and clinical pattern recognition.',
    clinicalImportance: 'Oculoplastics accounts for 6–10% of most exams. Lid malpositions, lacrimal sac pathology and thyroid eye disease are reliable recurring themes. FAICO oculoplasty candidates require deep operative knowledge.',
    anatomyRefresher: [
      'Upper lid retractors: levator palpebrae superioris (CN III) and Müller muscle (sympathetic).',
      'Lower lid retractors: capsulopalpebral fascia and inferior tarsal muscle.',
      'Whitnall ligament acts as a fulcrum for the levator.',
      'Lacrimal drainage: puncta → canaliculi → common canaliculus → lacrimal sac → nasolacrimal duct → inferior meatus (valve of Hasner).',
      'Orbital walls: medial (lamina papyracea) thinnest; floor (maxilla) common in blowout fracture.',
    ],
    highYieldFacts: [
      'Ptosis classification: aponeurotic, myogenic, neurogenic, mechanical, traumatic.',
      'Levator function grading: poor (<4 mm), fair (5–7 mm), good (8–12 mm), excellent (>12 mm).',
      'Surgical choice: good function — levator advancement; poor function — frontalis sling.',
      'Entropion: most often involutional (lower lid laxity); cicatricial in trachoma and SJS.',
      'Ectropion: involutional, cicatricial, paralytic (CN VII palsy), mechanical.',
      'Lagophthalmos management: lubricants, taping, gold weight implant, lateral tarsorrhaphy.',
      'Chalazion: chronic granulomatous inflammation of meibomian gland; treat with warm compresses, lid hygiene, I&C if persistent.',
      'Hordeolum: acute staphylococcal infection of meibomian (internal) or Zeiss (external) gland.',
      'Basal cell carcinoma: most common eyelid malignancy; pearly nodule with telangiectasia; medial canthus most common.',
      'Sebaceous gland carcinoma: masquerade as chronic chalazion; pagetoid spread, biopsy mandatory.',
      'Congenital nasolacrimal duct obstruction: epiphora from birth, resolves spontaneously by 12 months in most; probe at 12 months if persistent.',
      'Acute dacryocystitis: painful swelling over lacrimal sac; treat with systemic antibiotics, then DCR.',
      'External DCR vs endonasal DCR: external has slightly higher success but visible scar.',
      'Thyroid eye disease: upper lid retraction, lid lag (von Graefe), proptosis, EOM restriction (IR, MR most affected); IOSS or Hertel measurement.',
      'Blowout fracture: inferior rectus entrapment causes diplopia; oculocardiac reflex needs urgent surgery in white-eyed blowout.',
